{# ───────────────────────── Slide 11 — Calibration & honesty ───────────────────────── #}
<section class="slide" data-title="Calibration">
  <p class="eyebrow reveal"><span class="tick"></span>Volume 10 · Calibration & honesty</p>
  <h2 class="display reveal">Findings you can argue with.</h2>
  <div class="two-col" style="margin-top: 1.4rem;">
    <div class="reveal">
      <h3>Every finding is auditable.</h3>
      <p style="font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif; font-size: 0.96rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft);">
        Each row in the SQLite store carries the prompt version, prompt SHA-256, the model that produced it, the conversation ID, and the turn ID hash. You can reconstruct exactly what the judge saw.
      </p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">Null findings are not findings.</h3>
      <p style="font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif; font-size: 0.96rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft);">
        When a module says "no sycophancy here" or "no drift," that's an absence of signal, not a hit. The report's headline list filters them out. They appear in the per-module sections, where ran-clean is the right reading.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="reveal">
      <h3>Three calibration anchors.</h3>
      <ul style="padding-left: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--ink-soft); font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif; font-size: 0.96rem;">
        <li><strong>Module A (Spiral-Bench):</strong> calibrated against eqbench's reference labels via Krippendorff α + Gwet AC1.</li>
        <li><strong>Module C (SycEval):</strong> calibrated against the AAAI AIES 2025 progressive/regressive labels.</li>
        <li><strong>Module H (Memory):</strong> calibrated by hand-labelled subset; sub-claim refinement when the parent is too coarse.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-family: ui-serif, Georgia, serif; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--ink-soft); font-style: italic; line-height: 1.55; margin-top: 0.8rem;">
        Confidence intervals are Beta posteriors with BCa bootstrap. The whiskers in the report are not for show.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <aside class="speaker-notes">
    <strong>This is the slide that wins the technical judge.</strong> Calibration numbers, not vibes.
    <strong>Krippendorff α</strong> measures inter-rater agreement for ordinal labels; <strong>Gwet AC1</strong> is more robust when one label dominates (which it does in sycophancy). Per-label κ. Quadratic-weighted κ on intensity. BCa bootstrap for confidence intervals.
    <strong>The point:</strong> a finding's confidence value is the result of an actual statistical procedure, not a model's self-rated number.
  </aside>
  <div class="slide-chrome">
    <span class="runner">№ 11 · Calibration & honesty</span>
